 {"q": "Josephus says Titus tried to save the Temple. Why do historians treat this claim with caution?", "options": ["Because Josephus was not in Jerusalem at the time", "Because Josephus wrote under the patronage of Titus's family, and another ancient source, Sulpicius Severus, possibly drawing on Tacitus, says Titus chose to destroy it", "Because the Temple was never burned", "Because Josephus hated the Romans"], "answer": 1, "why": "Josephus was an eyewitness, but he had every reason to clear his patron."},
 {"q": "Why does the story of Jesus son of Ananus matter for the study of Jesus of Nazareth?", "options": ["Because they were the same person", "Because it shows that a prophet who spoke against the Temple could be beaten by the leaders and handed to the Roman governor, making the Gospel account more believable", "Because Josephus says he was a Christian", "Because he founded the church in Jerusalem"], "answer": 1, "why": "The parallel shows how Jerusalem's authorities dealt with prophets against the Temple, thirty years after Jesus."},
